1. ECDICT -- English-Chinese dictionary database
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Upstream:  https://github.com/skywind3000/ECDICT
   License:   MIT
   Holder:    Copyright (c) 2025 Linwei

   Chinese meanings, phonetic transcriptions, part-of-speech labels and
   inflected forms in the bundled runtime lexicon are derived from ECDICT's
   `translation`, `phonetic`, `pos` and `exchange` columns. The project
   filters the word set down to a primary-school classroom scope and
   reformats the text (stripping part-of-speech prefixes and bracketed
   notes, normalizing separators to the full-width semicolon), but the
   meanings and phonetics themselves originate upstream.

   Affected files:
     tools/lexicon-data/core-lexicon.source.json
     public/assets/lexicon/core-lexicon.json
     public/assets/lexicon/core-lexicon-extended.json

   Build step: tools/build-hfv-ecdict-core.mjs, then
               tools/build-runtime-lexicon.mjs

   Upstream chain: that repository's README states its database grew over
   years from assorted reference materials and contributed word lists, that
   it incorporated the open-source cdict dictionary data, and that it was
   proofread against the British National Corpus word list. ECDICT names no
   separate license for those portions; the repository-level MIT grant above
   is the one this project relies on.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. high-frequency-vocabulary -- ranked English word list
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Upstream:  https://github.com/arstgit/high-frequency-vocabulary
   License:   MIT
   Holder:    Copyright (c) 2018 Derek H

   The word list and its frequency ordering determine which entries the
   runtime lexicon includes and which learning band each word receives. Only
   `30k.txt` is read, and only the word and its rank are taken; the
   Chinese explanations in that repository's `30k-explained.txt` are not
   used and are not redistributed here.

   The `rank` field in tools/lexicon-data/core-lexicon.source.json carries
   this ordering. The raw upstream file is not committed.

   Upstream chain: that repository's README states its word list is derived
   from Peter Norvig's compilation of the 1/3 million most frequent English
   words (http://norvig.com/ngrams/), itself built from the Google Web
   Trillion Word Corpus. That compilation carries no license statement of
   its own; the MIT grant above is the one this project relies on.

